# Generated by python -m ridge.deck_narrative --write. Scenario wording is
# rendered from assets/scenario-narrative-v1.json; do not retype it here.
pageType: content
audience: participant
background:
  type: solid
  color: "$paper"
elements:
  - elementId: tag
    elementType: text
    bounds: [64, 40, 400, 16]
    content: {style: "$tag", wrap: false, text: PART 1 · THE INCIDENT}
  - elementId: title
    elementType: text
    bounds: [64, 66, 832, 34]
    content: {style: "$title", fontSize: 30, wrap: false, text: The incident brief}
  - elementId: rule
    elementType: line
    bounds: [64, 116, 832, 2]
    viewBox: [832, 2]
    points: "0,1.0 832,1.0"
    curve: sharp
    border: {style: solid, width: 2, color: "$line"}
  - elementId: premise
    elementType: text
    bounds: [64, 136, 832, 72]
    contractRef: exercise.premise
    content:
      style: "$body"
      fontSize: 15
      text: |
        <p>A fictional internal document service holds the movement briefs for patrol LANTERN, a fictional field unit. Those briefs are the kind of document an organisation genuinely protects: a sector, a movement window and a check-in word. At 09:30 UTC the outgoing shift hands your team an unfinished problem rather than an incident that has already been solved.</p>
  - elementId: discovery-head
    elementType: text
    bounds: [64, 236, 396, 19]
    content: {fontSize: 15, bold: true, color: "$slate", wrap: false, text: WHAT THE REVIEW FOUND}
  - elementId: discovery-rule
    elementType: line
    bounds: [64, 263, 396, 2]
    viewBox: [396, 2]
    points: "0,1.0 396,1.0"
    curve: sharp
    border: {style: solid, width: 2, color: "$slate"}
  - elementId: discovery
    elementType: text
    bounds: [64, 275, 396, 182]
    contractRef: exercise.discovery
    content:
      style: "$body"
      fontSize: 14
      text: |
        <p>It was not the document service that raised the alarm. An access review after a routine credential change noticed that one document had been fetched and re-used outside its normal pattern on the same morning, while the same session was refreshed from an address that appears in no approved remote-access list. Two colleagues both acted on the same weak signal at once, and neither escalated it. You inherit the gap between what they saw and what they never checked.</p>
  - elementId: exposure-head
    elementType: text
    bounds: [500, 236, 396, 19]
    content: {fontSize: 15, bold: true, color: "$amber", wrap: false, text: WHAT IS STILL UNKNOWN}
  - elementId: exposure-rule
    elementType: line
    bounds: [500, 263, 396, 2]
    viewBox: [396, 2]
    points: "0,1.0 396,1.0"
    curve: sharp
    border: {style: solid, width: 2, color: "$amber"}
  - elementId: exposure
    elementType: text
    bounds: [500, 275, 396, 136]
    contractRef: exercise.exposure
    content:
      style: "$body"
      fontSize: 14
      text: |
        <p>One movement brief is the supported exposure. A second, larger document was requested from the same session and refused. A third workstation showed a similar tool name and was never collected from. All three of those are different kinds of unknown, and the difference between them is the whole exercise.</p>
  - elementId: footer
    elementType: text
    bounds: [64, 505, 700, 15]
    content: {fontSize: 12, color: "#7E877F", wrap: false, text: OPERATION SILENT RIDGE — EXERCISE ONLY}
  - elementId: pagenum
    elementType: text
    bounds: [880, 505, 40, 15]
    content: {fontSize: 12, color: "#7E877F", align: [right, top], wrap: false, text: "2"}
